o potential pungs to aim for.\n\nBut the user can only discard one tile. Which one to discard?\n\nIf they discard a T3, they have T3x1, and can still form a pung if they get two more. But there are 4 T3 in the game, and they have 2, so discarding one leaves 1, needing two more. Alternatively, if they discard a W8, they have W8x1, needing two more. The same applies to F3.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discarding a tile that's less useful for forming chows. For example, if they keep W1 and W2, they can wait for W3 to form a chow. Similarly, if they have W6 and W8, they can wait for W7. Or T3x2 can wait for T4.\n\nWhich of these is more likely? It depends on the unshown tiles. Let's look at the unshown tiles:\n\nUnshown tiles are listed as [W1: 4; W2: 4; W3: 4; W4: 4; W5: 4; W6: 4; W7: 4; W8: 4; W9: 4; T1: 4; T2: 4; T3: 4; T4: 4; T5: 4; T6: 4; T7: 4; T8: 3; T9: 4; B1: 3; B2: 4; B3: 4; B4: 4; B5: 4; B6: 4; B7: 4; B8: 4; B9: 3; F1: 4; F2: 4; F3: 4; F4: 4; J1: 4; J2: 4; J3: 4].\n\nSo the unshown tiles for W3 is 4, W7 is 4, T4 is 4. So they are still available.\n\nIf the user keeps W1 and W2, the chance of drawing W3 is 4 in the wall. Similarly for W7. For T4, it's 4.\n\nSo which discard would give the most chances?\n\nIf the user discards a T3, they can wait for T3 (need two more). The unshown T3 is 4, so there are 4 T3 left. They have 2 in hand, so discarding one leaves 1 in hand, needing two more. The chance of drawing two T3 is lower than drawing one of W3 or W7.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard a T3, they can also form a chow with T3-T4-T5. So if they have T3x2, they can wait for T4 to form a chow. But if they discard one T3, they have T3x1, so they need T4 and T5 to form a chow (3-4-5), which requires two more tiles. Not as good as waiting for one tile.\n\nSimilarly for W8: if they have W8x2, they can wait for W7 to form 6-7-8. If they discard a W8, they have W8x1, needing W7 and W6 (but they have W6 already). Wait, they have W6 and W8, so if they keep W6 and W8, they can